WEBVTT

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:10.280
August 1947.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:18.800
The British are quitting India nearly 200 years after they took power.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:28.080
One of the largest, most ethnically diverse nations in the world has been divided.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.520
One country will now become two.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:32.040
India and Pakistan.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:41.880
As a British barrister draws a line on a map, a once peaceful land implodes.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:44.480
People are forced out of the villages they have lived in for generations.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.240
15 million scramble to be on the right side of the new border.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:53.200
At least 1 million die in the process.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:58.960
There was this huge mass of humanity just moving along.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.520
Barefooted, no food, thirsty.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:03.920
It was a terrible sight.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:12.360
Communities that have lived together for centuries, turn on each other in one of the worst communal massacres of the 20th century.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.800
It was so senseless, so utterly senseless.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.200
And yet there was nothing you could do about it.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:27.760
Britain, the once great colonial power, looks on as India burns.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:34.280
Hindus and Muslims were in the grip of madness, you know.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:05.320
In 1946, British India was largely at peace.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:16.160
On the surface, there was communal harmony between Hindus, Muslims and Sikhs.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:29.600
In the same part of your village, I remember Hindus and Sikhs living side by side, you know.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.960
Living with us, you know.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:31.800
Not far from where I lived, you know.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:39.760
And whenever there was any wedding in the family, I remember the Hindus and the Sikhs bringing sweetmeats to my father, you know.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:43.360
And we lived very happily.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.760
Very happily.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:55.200
In British India, the 255 million Hindus were in a majority.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.120
India's 92 million Muslims were concentrated in the north-west and north-east of the country.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:06.160
The 6 million Sikhs lived mostly in the Punjab, one of the richest and most diverse provinces in India.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:10.800
Lahore was its ancient capital.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:21.280
Lahore was one of the most cosmopolitan cities in the country.

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:25.360
A centre for education and fashion.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:42.040
Roeded Khan, a Muslim, had left his village to study law and history at Lahore's Foreman Christian College.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.280
Lahore took my breath away, you know.

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:48.960
It was a different city, you know.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:54.160
It was a different world.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:12.000
It was a very liberal, very tolerant, very progressive institution.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:24.840
At the same time, Som Anand, a Hindu, was in his final year of school in one of Lahore's fashionable suburbs.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.880
The fashions in Lahore became popular all over Punjab.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:38.480
Not only in Punjab but in northern India.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:55.520
There was a famous saying in Punjabi that a person who has not seen Lahore has not been born yet, is yet to be born.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:09.040
In a few months' time, the peace of this ancient city would be shattered.

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:16.600
For nearly 200 years, Britain ruled over India's 380 million people.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:23.160
India was the centrepiece of Britain's empire, a source of money and power.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:31.960
For the British who were stationed there, life had changed little in decades.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:46.480
John Moores, an officer to a Gurkha regiment, had been in India for a year.

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:47.840
We had a hunt.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:55.760
And they'd imported these hounds, possibly in the 20s or somewhere like that and they used to hunt jackal.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:06.840
The tribesmen would come in with a live jackal and release them eventually and then the hunt would take it up and try chasing them.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:14.240
And then you had to have a whole brigade out, defending the hunters.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:17.520
They would be deployed all round over so many miles.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:32.480
For decades, Indians had fought to rid themselves of British rule.

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:35.160
The independence movement had been kept in check by ruthless military force.

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:39.440
But by 1946, everything had changed.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:45.280
World War Two had left Britain bankrupt.

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:52.560
Mr Moores and the other British troops knew that they would be the last.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:07:06.560
We knew that having come through a very expensive World War, that the cost of maintaining an army in India following the Far East war... we realised that this couldn't be paid for.

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:07.360
The tax payer...

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:15.120
England was in a bad state, particularly 46, 47, financially.

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:24.600
But India's independence leaders were divided over what should happen when Britain left.

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:35.960
The Indian National Congress, under its leaders, Pandit Nehru and Mahatma Gandhi, demanded a single state where all religions would live side by side.

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:43.000
But many Muslims feared living under a Hindu majority.

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:47.800
Even in the middle class homes of Lahore, ancient prejudices ran deep.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:48.360
At one level they had very cordial relations.

00:07:49.480 --> 00:08:04.560
Our Muslim neighbours were very good, they helped us, we helped them, but Hindus had a curious... inhibitions.

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:20.520
My mother's attitude, she didn't allow any Muslim to enter her kitchen, any cooked thing from a Muslim home was not allowed to enter the kitchen.

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:27.760
When she was eating, she would not allow her Muslim neighbours, the lady, to touch her.

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:36.520
So such inhibitions and customs, they kept us apart.

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:53.840
In Lahore's old city, Muslims were forbidden from drinking from the same taps as Hindus.

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:08.480
Zahoor-Ud-Din, a Muslim, was a champion wrestler from the old city.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:16.600
The Muslims were not getting enough jobs, education.

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:26.760
They were not so education.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:33.720
They envied Hindus who had the best of jobs and they always wanted...

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:44.480
That's why the slogan of a Muslim homeland became very popular among the Muslims.

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:58.800
Muslim fears that Hindus would dominate an independent India drove the demand for a separate Muslim homeland.

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:11.240
It was championed principally by one man, a 70 year-old British-educated barrister, Muhammad Ali Jinnah.

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:14.600
His Muslim League demanded the division of India into two.

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:22.880
One country for the Hindus and Sikhs and another for the Muslims.

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:26.920
Once the British leave and there will be a democratic form of government, you see.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:34.480
But in a democratic form of government, the Hindus will always be in a majority and the Muslims will always be in a minority.

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:34.680
We have no chance.

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:38.440
Our minority will never be transformed into a majority, you know.

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:38.760
And the Hindu majority will always remain a permanent majority.

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:41.160
Therefore, we have no chance.

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:52.200
Pandit Nehru, the leader of Congress, toured the country, arguing the case for a united India.

00:10:55.040 --> 00:11:05.840
The whole idea that India should be divided was based on a fantastic notion that religion constitutes nationality.

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:12.920
Nayantara Sahgal lived with her uncle, Pandit Nehru.

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:32.960
While I was growing up, the entire national movement which had cut across religion, region, caste, class, sex, you know, it had been a truly unifying experience, so, for a person like me, it was an absurd idea that India should be divided.

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:37.080
Made no sense of any kind.

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:43.560
Clouds gather over Simla for the opening of Lord Wavell's conference with the Indian leaders.

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:51.400
In March 1946, a British Government delegation a Cabinet mission arrived to negotiate between Congress and the league.

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:57.280
This was supposed to be the breakthrough conference where a deal would be struck, leading to an independent India.

00:11:56.520 --> 00:12:05.160
Pandit Nehru, who calls for an Indian republic, is accused by the League of working for domination over the Muslim minority.

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:08.160
Jinnah did compromise on his demand for a separate Muslim state.

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:13.040
He accepted a united India if it had weak, central powers.

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:21.440
But Nehru, a socialist, and believer in a strong central state, refused any such concessions to the Muslim League.

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:28.600
Mr Jinnah, the Muslim leader, holds fast to Pakistan and it's reported that his maximum concessions were regarded by Congress as inadequate.

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:32.720
At any rate, the fateful conference at Simla, following the preliminary work in Delhi, has ended in failure.

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:42.360
Calcutta.

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:46.880
A stronghold of the Muslim League.

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:50.920
August 16th, 1946.

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:09.640
With the collapse of negotiations, Jinnah called for direct action.

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:16.000
Thousands of Muslims gathered in the centre of the city to demand a separate homeland, Pakistan.

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:36.640
As the crowds dispersed, the more radical elements headed for the Hindu parts of the city.

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:52.960
I was thinking of going out for a stroll.

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:58.760
And then, all of a sudden, I found some Muslim hoodlums.

00:13:57.800 --> 00:14:01.600
They came out, shouting...

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:13.920
"Allahu akbar" - Allah is the great master.

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:20.760
Local gangsters hijacked the demonstration.

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:24.640
They whipped up the mob and it turned violent.

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:34.600
Then I found that a neighbour who had come out to see what was happening, his head was cut.

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:39.720
And lay bleeding, so I thought that going out would be absolutely foolhardy.

00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:50.320
Then these boys, they started hitting the doors and they said that "We will not spare any Hindu."

00:14:52.760 --> 00:15:06.200
As the city erupted in communal violence, Muslim slaughter of Hindus turned to Hindu slaughter of Muslims.

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:10.000
Shankar Ghosh was covering the riots for a Calcutta newspaper.

00:15:14.200 --> 00:15:24.000
A jewellery shop was... surrounded by a crowd armed with iron rods.

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:30.520
And the elderly employee - he was already half-dead in fear.

00:15:29.720 --> 00:15:35.320
He came with his folded hands like this.

00:15:34.760 --> 00:15:45.600
But this furious mob - they had no time to hear what he wanted to say.

00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:51.600
I think a boy of 14 or 15 just pulled at his leg as he came out.

00:15:51.040 --> 00:16:02.160
And the man fell down and someone hit him on the head with an iron rod.

00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:06.680
I think that one blow finished him.

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:16.760
Around 5,000 people were killed in three days of rioting.

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:34.240
But the British, with one eye on leaving India, fearful of being overrun, ordered the troops to stay in their barracks until it was too late.

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:39.280
We had absolute faith in British authority.

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:44.080
We had absolute faith in the peace that the British had given us.

00:16:43.280 --> 00:16:49.080
For three days there was no sign of authority on the streets.

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:54.480
And anybody could murder anybody.

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:15.359
The Calcutta killings shattered the hope of the British that they could get out of India quietly and peacefully.

00:17:14.680 --> 00:17:22.920
With religious hatred and suspicion growing, the dream of a united India seemed to be falling apart.

00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:28.040
Pran Chopra was a young reporter for All India Radio at the time of the riots.

00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:36.920
Until then, there was still the possibility that there could be an undivided India.

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:49.200
That impression evaporated in the heat of what came to be known as the great Calcutta killings.

00:17:48.320 --> 00:18:00.040
Once that... impact spread through the rest of the country, the hope that India could remain undivided began to vanish.

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:03.800
After Calcutta, the violence spread.

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:07.080
In Bihar, Hindus largely massacred Muslims.

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:13.240
In Noakhali, in Bengal, it was Hindus who were mostly under attack.

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:18.080
With British power waning, India was in danger of falling apart in the chaos of communal violence.

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:30.160
One man thought he could bring the country together and maintain the dream of a united India - Mahatma Gandhi.

00:18:32.920 --> 00:18:41.320
Ashoka Gupta was one of Gandhi's disciples.

00:18:40.440 --> 00:19:12.920
Gandhi spent the first months of 1947 walking through the villages of Noakhali, holding prayer meetings, trying to bring Hindus and Muslims together.

00:19:15.040 --> 00:19:23.040
Ashoka Gupta went with him.

00:19:22.080 --> 00:20:09.320
Shankar Ghosh reported the story for his Calcutta newspaper.

00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:34.920
Gandhi had always opposed constitutional concessions to Muslims.

00:20:34.280 --> 00:20:39.400
For many of them, he represented Hindu supremacy.

00:20:38.920 --> 00:20:45.080
Mr Gandhi did not appeal to the Muslims, you know?

00:20:44.320 --> 00:20:50.680
He failed to win the hearts and minds of the Muslims of India.

00:20:50.480 --> 00:20:56.320
The way he used to talk about politics and his language, it was foreign.

00:20:55.520 --> 00:20:59.640
It did not appeal to Muslims at all.

00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:04.640
But he used to be practically, you know, naked.

00:21:04.440 --> 00:21:11.520
He used to have one arm on the shoulder of one girl, and he would have another arm on the shoulder of another.

00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:17.400
And the Hindus thought he was a saint, you know.

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:33.320
Gandhi's dream of a united India seemed to be collapsing.

00:21:32.600 --> 00:21:56.760
In February 1947, the British government made a dramatic declaration.

00:21:56.000 --> 00:22:04.040
They would definitely leave India by June 1948.

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:06.760
But there was still no agreement on how to give India to the Indians.

00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:12.640
With chaos spreading, they decided on a radical solution - to appoint a new Viceroy.

00:22:13.960 --> 00:22:23.760
The occasion - the departure of Lord Mountbatten of Burma, who was leaving for India with his wife.

00:22:22.920 --> 00:22:32.040
Before the new Viceroy there lay the task of holding office for the brief, but obviously difficult period of just over a year, until the date when Indians are to take full control of India.

00:22:31.240 --> 00:22:35.640
Everyone in Britain, I am certain, wishes the Viceroy all possible success in India.

00:22:39.800 --> 00:22:47.080
I was in school and they said, you will miss the last term of school.

00:22:46.240 --> 00:22:47.760
We are taking you out in the middle of this term because...

00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:50.240
Daddy's been appointed Viceroy of India.

00:22:49.280 --> 00:22:54.520
And so we're going out for probably a couple of years.

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:02.840
And, you know, we thought we'd take you as well because you can be useful!

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:13.960
In March 1947, Mountbatten arrived in India.

00:23:13.160 --> 00:23:20.680
The former Supreme Allied Commander of south-east Asia had a reputation for being a decisive leader.

00:23:19.840 --> 00:23:21.600
A man who could knock heads together.

00:23:20.840 --> 00:23:29.560
His brief was simple - to get a deal and get Britain out before India imploded.

00:23:30.040 --> 00:23:33.400
The swearing-in of course was a very great ceremony in the Durbar Hall.

00:23:32.720 --> 00:23:48.640
And so trumpeters, all the... staff in the full dress uniform, my father in full dress uniform, my mother in a long white dress and I think a sort of laurel wreath in her hair.

00:23:49.360 --> 00:23:53.320
And... they looked like film stars!

00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:14.640
The Viceroy, his wife, Edwina, the Vicereine, and their daughter Pamela, moved into Viceroy's House in New Delhi with its staff of 5,000.

00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:20.200
In Viceroy's House there were indoor gardeners.

00:24:19.520 --> 00:24:25.280
Now, the indoor gardeners just arranged the flowers in the vases and changed the water.

00:24:24.400 --> 00:24:28.200
And there were 25 of them as indoor gardeners!

00:24:27.640 --> 00:24:38.680
There was one man who spent his entire time with a seal that bore the British Crown, stamping the butter pats.

00:24:37.800 --> 00:24:43.960
And there was another unfortunate man known as the chicken plucker, who did nothing else!

00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:47.920
So the scale was so amazing.

00:24:49.800 --> 00:24:58.640
Narendra Singh Sarila was to become Mount Batten's aide de camp, his personal assistant.

00:24:57.880 --> 00:25:04.720
Among other things, his job was to assist on tiger hunts.

00:25:09.800 --> 00:25:15.480
Lord Mountbatten was a very articulate man.

00:25:14.560 --> 00:25:18.640
And he used to plan ahead what he was going to do.

00:25:18.040 --> 00:25:23.120
And he used to carry it through, ruthlessly.

00:25:22.880 --> 00:25:32.240
Despite the formalities of life at Viceroy's House, Mount Batten seemed like a new breed of Viceroy.

00:25:32.320 --> 00:25:35.280
First of all, his background helped him.

00:25:34.600 --> 00:25:39.440
Because everybody knew that he was cousin of the King.

00:25:38.600 --> 00:25:44.280
But still, he was... he acted without any airs.

00:25:43.360 --> 00:25:46.760
And I think that impressed Indians.

00:25:45.920 --> 00:25:54.560
Because other viceroys kept, you know, their upper-lip, and that sort of thing.

00:25:53.800 --> 00:25:58.560
And I think that helped to impress people.

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:08.800
Well the first thing that was different with my father and mother was that when they were entertaining, I mean, they were in India, they invited Indians, which had never happened before.

00:26:07.840 --> 00:26:14.280
I mean, yes, of course Indian princes were entertained.

00:26:13.360 --> 00:26:20.520
But the ordinary Indian... ministers and lawyers and doctors or whoever, had never been invited.

00:26:19.720 --> 00:26:24.600
So that was quite a new thing.

00:26:27.640 --> 00:26:37.520
On the personal level, Dickie was a very charming, very handsome, very lovable person.

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:46.160
Who met one very informally and, you know... when one was very young and rather nervous in front of important people, he could put one at one's ease.

00:26:45.400 --> 00:26:48.320
He was awfully sweet.

00:26:52.320 --> 00:26:57.760
Mountbatten's first task was to meet the Indian leaders.

00:26:56.920 --> 00:27:06.280
Mahatma Gandhi and Pandit Nehru for Congress and Mohammed Ali Jinnah for the Muslim League.

00:27:05.400 --> 00:27:15.560
He hoped his more informal style would give him greater success than his stuffier predecessors.

00:27:14.600 --> 00:27:23.840
They sat away from the desks and armchairs and were offered refreshments of some kind.

00:27:23.680 --> 00:27:25.160
And he would...

00:27:24.480 --> 00:27:34.520
You know, he would say, well now, before you put me in the picture politically on the state of India, do tell me a bit about yourself.

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:37.800
It was a technique that worked with Nehru.

00:27:36.920 --> 00:27:39.520
The two would become lifelong friends.

00:27:38.600 --> 00:27:41.600
But it totally failed with Jinnah.

00:27:40.880 --> 00:27:51.160
He came out of the meeting afterwards and said to his aides, "My goodness, he was cold!

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:51.440
Cold as ice!"

00:27:54.680 --> 00:28:03.520
There were now constant negotiations.

00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:20.720
As a divided India became a real possibility, the political tensions at the centre filtered down to the local level.

00:28:22.480 --> 00:28:30.160
The Punjab was worst affected.

00:28:29.360 --> 00:28:34.640
The provenance was one of the wealthiest in India.

00:28:33.760 --> 00:28:43.280
Hindus, Muslims and Sikhs all claimed it as their own.

00:28:44.480 --> 00:28:50.600
The Punjab was home to most of India's 6 million Sikhs.

00:28:49.680 --> 00:28:55.440
The location of their most holy site - the Golden Temple.

00:28:54.680 --> 00:29:07.840
The Sikhs great fear was that if India was divided, the Punjab, with its Muslim majority, would end up in Pakistan.

00:29:07.040 --> 00:29:10.960
For Sikhs, it was unthinkable that they would be ruled by the Muslims.

00:29:10.160 --> 00:29:14.680
Historically, their enemies.

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:25.680
In many villages of the Punjab, communal harmony began to break down.

00:29:24.840 --> 00:29:32.320
Aridaman Singh Dhillon was living in a mixed Sikh-Muslim village.

00:29:32.720 --> 00:29:37.080
I had Muslim friends and we used to play together.

00:29:36.280 --> 00:29:41.400
And there was nobody from our families put any obstacles between us.

00:29:40.560 --> 00:29:44.960
But then the strife began.

00:29:44.000 --> 00:29:51.720
Then I was prevented from going out with my Muslim friends.

00:29:50.960 --> 00:29:56.760
But we would sneak away and often would get caught and scolded.

00:29:56.000 --> 00:30:01.320
My grandfather, he noticed us going about, playing around in the fields.

00:30:01.280 --> 00:30:09.000
We tried to hide but ultimately he found out and I was given a good thrashing!

00:30:08.680 --> 00:30:11.640
And scolded.

00:30:11.720 --> 00:30:27.520
As a young boy, he watched local politicians stir up divisions between the communities for their own ends.

00:30:34.840 --> 00:30:49.880
They had to whip up the sentiments of the people and the easiest way to whip up of the sentiments of the people is to tell them that their religion is endangered.

00:30:49.000 --> 00:30:50.440
That their community is endangered.

00:30:49.760 --> 00:30:57.200
What else would they tell to hoodwink people, to get votes out of them, to get support out of them?

00:30:56.920 --> 00:30:59.640
The people had to be hoodwinked.

00:30:58.840 --> 00:31:04.200
And politicians do this.

00:31:03.400 --> 00:31:14.640
Tension also increased in Punjab's capital - Lahore.

00:31:13.760 --> 00:31:19.120
Student Som Anand always had a lot of Muslim friends.

00:31:18.360 --> 00:31:23.720
They now refused to talk to him.

00:31:24.000 --> 00:31:29.600
A class fellow of mine raised to the Congress flag on school building.

00:31:28.880 --> 00:31:31.640
The Muslims were very furious.

00:31:30.720 --> 00:31:37.200
They also raised their Muslim League flag on the school building.

00:31:36.400 --> 00:31:42.440
So the tension increased.

00:31:41.560 --> 00:31:44.920
The distrust was already there under the surface.

00:31:44.400 --> 00:31:53.040
It was there for centuries, but now it came above the surface.

00:31:52.360 --> 00:32:04.000
John Mores and his band of Ghurkas were now sent to Lahore to help the local police keep the peace.

00:32:03.560 --> 00:32:07.280
We arrived in Lahore round about the 23rd of April.

00:32:06.440 --> 00:32:12.920
We already knew that there were rumblings going on through intelligence that had been passed to us and we were briefed on that.

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:21.400
And our first job was to get to know the city and how to get around it.

00:32:26.040 --> 00:32:36.680
We had orders that we were to stay armed, and you would have to go to a dance or have dinner out somewhere with a great 45 revolver strapped on.

00:32:35.960 --> 00:32:48.160
Because there was already talk about people having reprisals.

00:32:52.280 --> 00:33:00.440
In the villages of the Punjab, there were few British patrols.

00:32:59.640 --> 00:33:05.240
The Sikhs began forming jathas, armed bands.

00:33:04.440 --> 00:33:13.360
Many Sikhs had served in the British Army during the war and still had their weapons.

00:33:12.680 --> 00:33:25.640
These ex-soldiers began to train their Sikh brothers to prevent a Muslim takeover of the Punjab.

00:33:24.720 --> 00:33:31.440
Singh had recently been released from prison where he had served time for assault.

00:33:44.280 --> 00:33:45.400
He lived in a Sikh majority part of the Punjab.

00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:15.000
A number of Muslims from our village had come down to see my grandfather.

00:34:14.040 --> 00:34:28.800
And told him that they had heard rumours that certain people are getting together in adjoining villages to attack our village and other villages who killed the Muslims.

00:34:27.920 --> 00:34:31.800
Singh Dhillon came from a long line of Sikh warriors.

00:34:31.040 --> 00:34:33.960
His ancestors had been imprisoned by the British.

00:34:33.040 --> 00:34:40.239
His grandfather, the village head, refused to join in the wave of communal violence.

00:34:41.120 --> 00:34:53.639
A committee of Sikh, Hindu and Muslim... people was created, and they were given small arms like lances and axes.

00:34:52.880 --> 00:34:58.000
We had a couple of guns.

00:34:57.120 --> 00:35:08.360
And the basic reason for doing this was to guard their own village against the people coming to loot the Muslims of that village.

00:35:07.640 --> 00:35:11.440
And to guard their Muslim friends.

00:35:10.560 --> 00:35:21.960
And every night they were patrolling throughout the village to guard the village and the Muslims.

00:35:22.720 --> 00:35:30.280
Seeing the Sikh preparations for war, Muslim villages decided to launch a first strike.

00:35:29.520 --> 00:35:44.480
In March 1947, Sikh villages around Rawalpindi came under attack.

00:36:09.480 --> 00:36:15.240
The Sikhs were heavily outnumbered by Muslims.

00:36:14.280 --> 00:36:22.600
Their biggest fear was that their women would be taken away, converted, and raped.

00:36:45.280 --> 00:36:48.800
The women of the village went into hiding.

00:36:48.160 --> 00:37:03.000
Singh's father decided to act to save the honour of his village.

00:37:15.840 --> 00:37:27.040
His teenage son looked on.

00:37:47.640 --> 00:38:51.880
Such scenes were repeated throughout the western Punjab in March and April 1947.

00:38:56.040 --> 00:39:02.720
With the threat of all-out conflict, a deal became a matter of urgency.

00:39:03.960 --> 00:39:10.480
The Congress leader, Pandit Nehru, now had power within his grasp.

00:39:09.800 --> 00:39:14.240
But to get the British out quickly, he had to compromise.

00:39:13.760 --> 00:39:20.840
He abandoned his dream of a united India and accepted partition.

00:39:21.400 --> 00:39:31.480
A statement will be read to you tonight, giving the final decision of his Majesty's government as to the method by which power will be transferred from British to Indian hands.

00:39:30.760 --> 00:39:42.800
On the night of 3rd June, 1947, Mountbatten, Jinnah and Nehru broadcast the news that they'd finally struck a deal - they would divide India in two.

00:39:44.120 --> 00:39:47.120
I most earnestly appeal to every community.

00:39:46.440 --> 00:39:53.120
And particularly Muslim India to maintain peace and order.

00:39:52.240 --> 00:39:53.400
I couldn't believe it.

00:39:52.440 --> 00:39:56.680
I couldn't believe it.

00:39:55.960 --> 00:39:57.840
I...

00:39:56.880 --> 00:40:03.080
I... it was like a dream come true, you know.

00:40:02.640 --> 00:40:15.040
Mighty forces are at work in the world today and in India, and I have no doubt that we are ushering in a period of greatness for India.

00:40:15.560 --> 00:40:19.280
I remember I heaved a sigh of relief that there was partition.

00:40:18.520 --> 00:40:24.200
Because we did not want to live with the Muslims any more.

00:40:24.480 --> 00:40:34.360
But there can be no question of coercing any large areas in which one community has a majority to live against their will under a government in which another community has a majority.

00:40:35.360 --> 00:40:45.320
The partition deal involved giving those provinces with a Muslim majority to Pakistan and those with a Hindu one to India.

00:40:44.800 --> 00:40:53.920
But Mountbatten and Nehru insisted that the Punjab and Bengal would be treated differently, despite their Muslim majorities.

00:40:53.200 --> 00:41:00.640
Jillah was forced to accept that both these provinces would be cut in half and divided between India and Pakistan.

00:41:00.160 --> 00:41:06.600
The next day, at a press conference, Mountbatten dropped a bombshell.

00:41:05.760 --> 00:41:14.920
Britain would not be leaving in June 1948, as had been planned, but on 15th August, 1947, just three months away.

00:41:14.200 --> 00:41:20.360
When asked why the date had been brought forward by nearly a year, Mountbatten replied, "Why should we wait?

00:41:19.600 --> 00:41:26.720
"Waiting would mean that I should be responsible, ultimately, for law and order."

00:41:28.000 --> 00:41:31.440
It should have been possible for them to say, look, there is no great hurry.

00:41:30.960 --> 00:41:34.080
Don't fix a timetable.

00:41:33.120 --> 00:41:34.480
Let it proceed in a more orderly manner.

00:41:33.520 --> 00:41:39.840
But he was a... a decisive man.

00:41:39.160 --> 00:41:47.720
This is the job I have to do - this is the date by which I'll do it.

00:41:46.960 --> 00:41:51.680
This was his personality.

00:41:50.760 --> 00:41:57.360
That made the pace of the partition furious, impossible to moderate.

00:41:59.440 --> 00:42:02.880
There was no question of it being too soon.

00:42:02.000 --> 00:42:04.320
It was much too late!

00:42:03.760 --> 00:42:16.120
Because in fact, when he arrived, as he saw it, the situation was so much more volcanic than he had been led to believe in England.

00:42:16.240 --> 00:42:20.320
And he had that diary that he had over everybody's desk, saying however many days to independence.

00:42:19.440 --> 00:42:21.440
Because it seemed so unbelievable.

00:42:21.360 --> 00:42:26.760
And the next morning you went into your office, it was one day less.

00:42:25.880 --> 00:42:29.080
And it certainly concentrated people's minds.

00:42:34.880 --> 00:42:41.400
But with three months to go, no decision had been made on one crucial matter.

00:42:40.800 --> 00:42:47.440
Where the border between Pakistan and India would lie.

00:42:46.560 --> 00:42:49.520
A new boundary had to be urgently drawn up.

00:42:48.640 --> 00:43:00.120
The man chosen for the task had never been east of Paris.

00:42:59.160 --> 00:43:00.320
British barrister Sir Cyril Radcliffe.

00:42:59.480 --> 00:43:05.880
Sir Radcliffe was a very remarkable man.

00:43:06.280 --> 00:43:14.240
It was not just the quality of his mind, though that was formidable, perhaps the most formidable I ever encountered, I think.

00:43:13.520 --> 00:43:20.800
He had this unerring gift for going to the heart of anything he was discussing.

00:43:19.960 --> 00:43:25.320
He came very clearly to a rational conclusion.

00:43:24.640 --> 00:43:27.080
He was a very rational man.

00:43:26.120 --> 00:43:31.920
On July eighth, Radcliffe arrived in India.

00:43:32.560 --> 00:43:39.200
He moved into a bungalow on the Viceroy's estate.

00:43:38.360 --> 00:43:44.600
With partition only 36 days away, he met Mountbatten.

00:43:43.680 --> 00:43:49.760
The brilliant and serious barrister did not quite hit it off with the flamboyant viceroy.

00:43:50.960 --> 00:43:56.080
I think he thought that he was a vain man.

00:43:55.160 --> 00:43:56.640
Erm...

00:43:55.680 --> 00:44:00.520
And not a man whose judgment was totally to be relied upon.

00:44:00.800 --> 00:44:05.120
But he didn't, he didn't dislike him.

00:44:04.240 --> 00:44:12.720
And I think he respected the fact that Mountbatten was the last Viceroy and doing the best he could with a very difficult remit.

00:44:14.200 --> 00:44:19.720
Could you get me the figures, please, for this district?

00:44:18.800 --> 00:44:24.080
I believe I'm right in saying that there is a narrow Muslim majority, but not a substantial one.

00:44:23.280 --> 00:44:27.440
Oh yes, 50.6.

00:44:26.640 --> 00:44:31.840
With his private secretary, Christopher Beaumont, Radcliffe began his work.

00:44:31.240 --> 00:44:39.360
His toughest job would be in the Punjab and Bengal, which were being divided in two.

00:44:38.520 --> 00:44:45.120
Radcliffe was being asked to draw a line through the middle of both provinces.

00:44:44.360 --> 00:44:58.040
In doing so, he would look at which religion was in the majority in each individual district, but he would also take into account other factors such as the connection of railways, canals, and irrigation channels.

00:44:59.680 --> 00:45:13.840
In the streets of its great capital, Lahore, there was uncertainty over which side of the city the border would fall.

00:45:13.080 --> 00:45:23.440
Satish Gujral was an art student in Lahore, from a politically-influential Hindu family.

00:46:15.480 --> 00:46:23.560
On the quayside at Bombay, Viscount Mountbatten bids bon voyage to 1,500 British soldiers.

00:46:22.640 --> 00:46:30.320
With tension increasing, the British government decided this was the moment to bring most of the remaining troops home.

00:46:36.560 --> 00:46:47.480
With just over a month to partition, tension on the streets of Lahore began to turn to violence.

00:46:47.400 --> 00:46:52.320
There was a date and everybody then knew that...

00:46:51.400 --> 00:46:57.120
Do whatever you can now, because after this date you won't be able to do it.

00:46:56.560 --> 00:47:03.440
Whether it was looting Muslims, looting Hindus, whatever it might be.

00:47:02.480 --> 00:47:10.680
So therefore... the date itself...became a driver.

00:47:10.560 --> 00:47:21.880
With the borders still to be decided, Muslims, Sikhs and Hindus began to clear their neighbourhoods of anyone not of their community.

00:47:29.040 --> 00:47:33.320
Despite violence erupting around the Punjab, the British presence was now minimal.

00:48:07.280 --> 00:48:11.720
John Moores and his small group of Gurkhas did what they could in Lahore.

00:48:10.800 --> 00:48:16.000
You tried to get in there quickly and say, "Well, what's happened with the police?"

00:48:15.080 --> 00:48:17.960
They'd say, "Well this shop's been set on fire."

00:48:17.200 --> 00:48:23.000
And the next thing would be someone would be killed or burnt.

00:48:22.080 --> 00:48:34.480
And so you then you'd get a reprisal from the other side then picking on somebody else and gradually this was how it escalated, it was like a bushfire and it was difficult to control.

00:48:37.400 --> 00:48:47.160
As Lahore tore itself apart, Mountbatten had other problems to solve.

00:48:46.440 --> 00:48:55.480
Partition was just a few weeks away, but the future of large parts of India had yet to be decided, that of the princely states.

00:48:54.880 --> 00:49:02.120
Ruled by princes, the semi-autonomous states were dotted around India.

00:49:01.600 --> 00:49:07.920
They had been allowed to run their own affairs in return for their loyalty to the British crown.

00:49:07.200 --> 00:49:20.200
The princes assumed the British would allow them to decide their own fate after the British had left, but Mountbatten had done a secret deal with Nehru.

00:49:19.480 --> 00:49:24.720
He would hand most of them over to India.

00:49:24.600 --> 00:49:31.280
Narendra Singh Sarila, then a young prince, attended the meeting where Mountbatten announced their future.

00:49:32.520 --> 00:49:45.560
In July '47, my father said that the last chamber of princes meeting is going to take place in Delhi, in which Mountbatten will address the princes.

00:49:44.720 --> 00:49:45.960
It was an important meeting.

00:49:45.000 --> 00:49:47.880
"You go, you deputise for me."

00:49:47.160 --> 00:49:49.400
He was trying to build me up.

00:49:48.480 --> 00:49:58.960
On July 25th, the princes gathered to hear what Mountbatten was offering.

00:50:01.320 --> 00:50:13.520
He came and stood in front, on the podium, and I remember that he looked left and right while pictures were taken.

00:50:12.760 --> 00:50:16.360
It was perfect showmanship.

00:50:15.400 --> 00:50:19.840
And then he started addressing the princes.

00:50:19.480 --> 00:50:28.800
"Your Highnesses, ladies and gentlemen..." and he said that we have no alternative but to sign the accession.

00:50:27.840 --> 00:50:36.840
Mountbatten told the princes they had little choice - Join the Indian Union or be swept away in a wave of democratic change.

00:50:37.200 --> 00:50:45.560
And I think this made a great impression and many of the princes who had this idea that they would become independent and do what they liked, they just collapsed.

00:50:44.960 --> 00:50:54.040
They realised that if they didn't sign... most of the princes, the small ones, would be most probably wiped out.

00:50:54.160 --> 00:51:01.960
With the princes in the bag, the partition settlement was nearly complete.

00:51:01.200 --> 00:51:10.480
All that remained was the final border, down to Sir Cyril Radcliffe.

00:51:09.920 --> 00:51:14.840
I think it was the loneliness of the decision which I remember him talking about.

00:51:14.160 --> 00:51:15.880
It wasn't a single decision.

00:51:14.920 --> 00:51:19.440
It was a series of decisions about where the line should be drawn.

00:51:18.600 --> 00:51:22.560
These decisions were going to be his and he had to take responsibility for them.

00:51:21.760 --> 00:51:28.960
I think he found that a lonely experience by comparison with some of the things he'd done.

00:51:29.640 --> 00:51:36.640
Sir Cyril had no time to see what was happening in the lands he was dissecting.

00:51:37.240 --> 00:51:52.600
His approach to drawing up the boundary separating majority Hindu - Sikh areas from majority Muslim ones almost certainly encouraged the religious cleansing of entire districts.

00:51:52.760 --> 00:51:56.440
There were areas where the margins were not very great.

00:51:55.640 --> 00:52:04.760
Therefore, the majority in those areas wanted to make sure that the process didn't get fudged with small numbers.

00:52:04.160 --> 00:52:16.440
So drive those, the minority, out, whether it is Hindu or Muslim minority, to make sure that this bit of territory doesn't slip out of your hands.

00:52:18.040 --> 00:52:24.240
Mayhem now ensued in the villages of the Punjab.

00:52:23.360 --> 00:52:26.440
A few individuals took a stand.

00:52:25.640 --> 00:52:33.640
Dhillon's grandfather was threatened for trying to protect his Muslim neighbours.

00:52:35.360 --> 00:52:49.720
Some people had often sent messages to my grandfather that people who were going about looting and killing Muslims, they wanted to eliminate our family because we were putting an obstacle in their way.

00:52:49.000 --> 00:52:56.440
But my grandfather would not be deterred, he would not be afraid.

00:53:05.360 --> 00:53:13.720
He was that way.

00:53:55.600 --> 00:54:11.240
Small contingents of British-led troops scoured the countryside trying to stop the violence.

00:54:19.920 --> 00:54:34.640
But by then, there were just too few to make a difference.

00:54:35.960 --> 00:54:43.520
One had messages coming in all the time, by hand - written messages.

00:54:42.840 --> 00:54:56.920
I can remember one, it was rather a sad, pathetic note by some chap who could write a little bit of English, and he had been in the Indian Navy and on a scrap of paper, this came in one day saying, "I'm home on leave at this particular village.

00:54:55.960 --> 00:54:59.680
"My name is so-and-so, my number is so-and-so.

00:54:58.960 --> 00:55:02.320
"Please can you come and help us?

00:55:01.640 --> 00:55:05.280
We are going to be attacked."

00:55:06.120 --> 00:55:14.840
But by the time we located the village, we got there and the village had been attacked and I couldn't find this man.

00:55:13.960 --> 00:55:17.440
A lot of people were killed.

00:55:17.320 --> 00:55:19.000
So it was that sort of message.

00:55:18.400 --> 00:55:23.680
It was sad, you know - people desperate for help and knew where we were.

00:55:22.840 --> 00:55:27.480
But when I say "we were", we were only 100 people.

00:55:26.680 --> 00:55:35.920
The whole area was something like 40,000 to 50,000 square miles.

00:55:35.240 --> 00:55:40.360
One village I went into where we knew there was something afoot.

00:55:39.680 --> 00:55:50.480
I hadn't been told to go there, I was coming back at the end of a patrol and I saw vultures circling, and so that's usually a pretty good indicator.

00:55:49.680 --> 00:55:57.640
I got into the village and sure enough, it had been completely decimated.

00:55:59.880 --> 00:56:04.960
The well was full of bodies, women.

00:56:04.000 --> 00:56:10.040
One woman had been pregnant and she'd been carved open completely and her breasts had been cut off.

00:56:09.240 --> 00:56:13.440
Awful, awful atrocities.

00:56:42.160 --> 00:56:47.480
In Lahore, murder was now an everyday occurrence.

00:56:46.680 --> 00:56:53.800
With partition just two weeks away, rumours circulated that the city would go to Pakistan.

00:56:52.960 --> 00:56:58.920
Lahore's richer Hindus took no chances.

00:56:58.920 --> 00:57:03.200
There was tension.

00:57:02.400 --> 00:57:11.160
The Hindus started leaving much before the partition in Lahore.

00:57:10.320 --> 00:57:13.800
They knew that Lahore would go to Pakistan.

00:57:13.120 --> 00:57:21.400
They started leaving the city, but they pretended they are not leaving.

00:57:22.480 --> 00:57:31.760
They kept their drawing rooms as it was - all the sofas and all the furniture was there.

00:57:30.960 --> 00:57:42.360
In their hearts, there was a hope lingered that Lahore would be on this side.

00:57:50.520 --> 00:58:08.600
Som's father - a bank manager in Lahore - said if they could live under the British, they could live under the Muslims.

00:58:08.080 --> 00:58:11.400
I didn't want to leave.

00:58:10.720 --> 00:58:13.800
My father also didn't want to leave.

00:58:13.040 --> 00:58:21.800
He hoped and he said, "All these people who are leaving are fools, they will come back tomorrow."

00:58:21.560 --> 00:58:24.280
But that was not true.

00:58:24.120 --> 00:58:33.680
When our neighbours started leaving, one by one, he still hoped.

00:58:32.920 --> 00:58:39.080
Our closest neighbours - who... we had a very close relationship - departed, yes.

00:58:38.120 --> 00:58:43.480
When they left, my father wept that day.

00:58:45.840 --> 00:58:57.600
As thousands fled, Muslim gangs set fire to the Sikh and Hindu areas of the old city.

00:58:56.680 --> 00:59:04.160
The old Lahore, beautiful and historic, was in flames.

00:59:03.480 --> 00:59:13.800
At night, when I used to sleep on the terrace, I could see the Hindu Shalmi area burning, five miles away.

00:59:13.120 --> 00:59:22.120
The flames could be seen, so high the flames were.

00:59:21.320 --> 00:59:25.840
Shalmi was a thickly populated Hindu area.

00:59:25.040 --> 00:59:30.840
It was burned down completely.

00:59:30.080 --> 00:59:38.800
And I could see five miles away the flames, the light of that, and we were so terrified that night.

01:00:16.160 --> 01:00:24.720
The whole horizon, full of fire and smoke, all red.

01:00:23.920 --> 01:00:32.960
It was such a sad, sad, sad moment for my life, seeing Lahore, such a beautiful city, all burning.

01:00:32.360 --> 01:00:46.960
I have done what I can in drawing the line to eliminate any avoidable cutting of railway communications and of river systems, which are...

01:00:46.200 --> 01:00:59.640
On August 9th, Radcliffe had finished drawing up the border, but Mountbatten decided to keep the new boundary a secret until after independence so the British wouldn't be blamed for any ensuing violence.

01:00:59.120 --> 01:01:07.280
The delay prolonged the uncertainty and some believe it increased the loss of life.

01:01:06.480 --> 01:01:24.320
..Signed Cyril Radcliffe.

01:01:23.360 --> 01:01:43.640
On the eve of independence, Radcliffe wrote to his step-son in London.

01:01:42.720 --> 01:01:51.400
"Nobody in India will love me for my award about the Punjab and Bengal, "and there will be roughly 80 million people with a grievance who will begin looking for me.

01:01:50.560 --> 01:01:53.640
"I do not want them to find me.

01:01:52.760 --> 01:01:57.240
"I have worked and travelled and sweated.

01:01:56.360 --> 01:02:00.120
"Oh, how I have sweated the whole time."

01:01:59.320 --> 01:02:07.040
Radcliffe left India four days later, never to return.

01:02:09.280 --> 01:02:24.680
He was deeply conscious that his rulings, his recommendations, would generate controversy, conflict, and the possibility of a loss of a lot of human life.

01:02:23.880 --> 01:02:37.720
I do remember him saying that...any solution, any drawing of the line, would have done that.

01:02:36.840 --> 01:02:44.680
On August 14th, 1947, a new country was born on the sub-continent.

01:02:43.920 --> 01:02:53.760
Jinnah's dream of a Muslim homeland became a reality in Pakistan.

01:02:57.840 --> 01:03:04.200
'Through Karachi's streets drives the Qaide-Azam, Mohammed Ali Jinnah, Pakistan's first Governor-General.

01:03:03.360 --> 01:03:07.840
'It's Karachi's first day of independence and the crowds are out to greet him and Earl Mountbatten.'

01:03:06.880 --> 01:03:12.920
Mountbatten was in Karachi to declare independence for the newly-created state.

01:03:12.240 --> 01:03:23.280
Bizarrely, no-one except he and Radcliffe knew where its borders were, but this didn't stop the celebrations.

01:03:26.520 --> 01:03:38.680
It never occurred to us that the Muslims of India will ever see the emergence of a sovereign, independent country which they could call their own, you see?

01:03:39.200 --> 01:03:45.600
A country they could live for, a country they could die for, you see?

01:03:44.800 --> 01:03:48.160
It never occurred to us.

01:03:47.760 --> 01:03:54.080
The next day, it was the turn of India to celebrate.

01:04:01.440 --> 01:04:16.360
Hundreds of thousands thronged the streets as Nehru, Mountbatten, his wife Edwina, and their daughter Pamela, made their way to the constituent assembly.

01:04:15.480 --> 01:04:28.040
Unfortunately, when my parents arrive, they can't get in because the crowd is so thick, so thick, that they can't open the doors, so there's no way for them to get in.

01:04:27.360 --> 01:04:39.400
So, somebody has to crawl in some window and go to the Prime Minister and say, "Slight difficulty, the Viceroy "can't get in because the crowd is so vast.

01:04:38.520 --> 01:04:40.720
Will you come and sort it out?"

01:04:39.880 --> 01:04:51.680
So Nehru had to sort of scuttle round the back and start hitting people and shouting at people and clearing away, which he was always brilliant at, and loved doing.

01:04:51.120 --> 01:04:58.160
And eventually, my parents were allowed to make their state entrance, considerably less dignified than had been expected.

01:05:13.400 --> 01:05:22.320
Well, you must remember that whatever the tragedy of partition, there are other aspects to it, and it was an exhilarating occasion.

01:05:21.440 --> 01:05:26.000
There were millions of people out on the streets celebrating.

01:05:25.200 --> 01:05:44.400
For my uncle, and for his colleagues, it was the realisation, though not in whole, but in part, of their lifelong dream - the cause to which they had given their entire lives.

01:05:43.440 --> 01:05:47.680
And here it was, Indian independence.

01:05:49.120 --> 01:05:53.200
But not everyone was celebrating.

01:05:52.520 --> 01:06:01.560
Gandhi, who for decades had struggled for a united, independent India, lay fasting in Calcutta.

01:06:00.680 --> 01:06:04.400
His disciple, Ashoka Gupta, was with him.

01:06:26.440 --> 01:06:41.120
With the celebrations over, Mountbatten finally announced the new border.

01:06:40.320 --> 01:06:48.720
It sliced through the previously united provinces of Bengal and Punjab.

01:06:47.920 --> 01:06:55.880
Lahore was given to Pakistan, a disaster for the city's few remaining Hindus.

01:06:55.240 --> 01:07:10.840
We were dejected and we were so sorry, because, you see, those Hindus bide their time, Hindus had realised that it would not be possible for them to stay in Pakistan.

01:07:16.560 --> 01:07:20.400
Millions of people were now on the move.

01:07:19.560 --> 01:07:32.440
Muslims who found themselves on the Indian side of the border and Hindus and Sikhs who were on the Pakistani side.

01:07:31.600 --> 01:07:46.800
They packed their belongings onto bullock carts and left the villages they had lived in for generations.

01:07:52.800 --> 01:08:32.760
The refugees formed human caravans, called kafilas.

01:08:34.319 --> 01:08:42.640
The lucky ones had military escorts to protect them from armed bands waiting on the roads.

01:08:43.640 --> 01:09:05.560
The first major refugee movement, the biggest concentration, was about a 50-miles-long column of over 200,000 people, and I was escorting it up to the Pakistan border.

01:09:04.800 --> 01:09:09.840
Wajahat Husain was a young army officer.

01:09:09.040 --> 01:09:15.040
He had been sent to the Punjab to deal with disturbances on the border.

01:09:14.200 --> 01:09:17.080
We had very few troops with us, very few troops.

01:09:16.560 --> 01:09:19.240
Here were these people.

01:09:18.359 --> 01:09:29.760
There were no transport arrangements, there were no arrangements for the medical cover, there was nothing, except that we had collected these people in open spaces.

01:09:29.000 --> 01:09:34.720
They were told to move, and they were moving on foot.

01:09:33.960 --> 01:09:36.319
Some had bullock carts.

01:09:35.439 --> 01:09:41.120
They had put their belongings on the bullock carts and the old people and children.

01:09:40.399 --> 01:09:42.720
Others were on foot.

01:09:43.000 --> 01:09:54.479
Initially, we used to do about 15 to 20 miles in a day, but as they got tired and the days passed by, they would do less and less.

01:10:31.120 --> 01:10:41.560
Throughout August and September, the columns of refugees grew and grew, from hundreds of thousands to millions.

01:10:41.040 --> 01:10:50.400
If you imagine two million people on one road, virtually, and it's just a seething mass of people trying to get water, trying to...

01:10:49.560 --> 01:10:52.280
Wells were poisoned.

01:10:51.360 --> 01:10:54.760
Trying to find food, trying to cope, trying to cook.

01:10:53.960 --> 01:10:57.360
Women having children.

01:10:56.520 --> 01:11:04.040
Awful, awful things.

01:11:06.800 --> 01:11:17.640
And of course they often tried to stop us and ask for help and sometimes, depending on what we were doing, we'd stop and say, "What's the matter?

01:11:16.840 --> 01:11:18.640
What do you want?"

01:11:18.240 --> 01:11:25.640
And they'd say, "We've no food," "I'm sorry, I can't do anything for you," because that wasn't our job.

01:11:24.760 --> 01:11:34.040
We just didn't have any food to give them.

01:11:44.040 --> 01:11:57.560
Plus they used to start throwing their baggage that they were carrying, whatever little they had to carry, after three or four days they used to gradually drop and then they were left with just the clothes tattered.

01:11:56.880 --> 01:12:06.920
And lastly, the mothers leaving their small babies, that was our biggest problem.

01:12:05.960 --> 01:12:15.440
And there were these crawling babies and on the side of the road with these thousands and thousands of people just moving.

01:12:14.680 --> 01:12:23.960
A terrible sight, a terrible sight.

01:12:28.440 --> 01:12:36.400
Tens of thousands of people boarded special refugee trains which made their way between India and Pakistan.

01:12:35.600 --> 01:12:40.400
They were easy prey for the armed gangs that roamed the countryside.

01:12:39.600 --> 01:12:48.920
It was John Moores' job to escort a trainload of Muslim refugees to Pakistan.

01:12:49.640 --> 01:12:59.040
It wasn't a train, it was just a lot of coal wagons, steel coal wagons, and this, of course, was August, which was pretty hot.

01:12:58.320 --> 01:13:06.720
And we loaded up these wretched refugees.

01:13:05.760 --> 01:13:11.800
It was very distressing.

01:13:11.000 --> 01:13:22.000
And the journey from Ambala to Amritsar by train normally you could do it, I suppose, in about four or five hours.

01:13:21.640 --> 01:13:26.360
And in this case it took four days.

01:13:37.000 --> 01:13:40.920
There was no water, very little water on the way.

01:13:39.960 --> 01:13:49.280
We had our own supplies of water for the three platoons, but we had a job to do and we couldn't help it, help them.

01:14:06.800 --> 01:14:16.200
Three days into their journey, they reached the Sikh stronghold of Amritsar.

01:14:15.520 --> 01:14:20.520
Just 14 miles from the border they were forced to stop to pick up water.

01:14:19.720 --> 01:14:21.480
The station had been taken over by hundreds of armed Sikhs.

01:14:22.480 --> 01:14:28.800
Amritsar station was crawling, it was packed, like sardines.

01:14:27.960 --> 01:14:32.720
Right across not just the platforms but across the tracks.

01:14:31.880 --> 01:14:42.680
They all had weapons of some sort or another, some were old blunderbusses, some were fairly modern rifles, spears, swords.

01:14:41.920 --> 01:14:48.480
They were shoulder to shoulder in crowds, pushing and shouting and screaming.

01:14:47.600 --> 01:14:55.680
You realised that you were just going into a hell and that if you didn't look tough, you were in for trouble.

01:14:54.880 --> 01:15:02.720
Because they would then have gone for us, before the refugees.

01:15:02.040 --> 01:15:09.600
I said, the thing to do is... to get the grenades out, let them see.

01:15:08.680 --> 01:15:13.760
A lot of them are ex-soldiers, these people here, the weapons they were carrying.

01:15:13.040 --> 01:15:14.400
Let them see you prime the grenades.

01:15:13.440 --> 01:15:19.120
Prime the grenades and they will know you mean business then.

01:15:18.240 --> 01:15:24.360
And doing that, the crowd sort of melted back and away and left us clear.

01:15:23.440 --> 01:15:28.320
And I suppose we must have been in the station for about four hours, something like that.

01:15:29.360 --> 01:15:38.000
And just as dawn was breaking, we managed to pull away the engine, to do the last 14 miles.

01:15:42.720 --> 01:15:49.120
There had been very little planning for the movement of refugees or their protection.

01:15:48.520 --> 01:15:59.560
Most British troops had already been sent home, whilst Indian and Pakistani forces were in total disarray.

01:15:59.160 --> 01:16:03.160
They were totally inadequate forces.

01:16:02.280 --> 01:16:04.200
The reason was very simple.

01:16:03.360 --> 01:16:07.360
Because the Indian Army was being split.

01:16:06.600 --> 01:16:14.400
At the same time, don't forget, that the troops were also concerned about their families.

01:16:13.640 --> 01:16:17.920
Because wherever they came from, the trouble was going on in their homes also.

01:16:17.040 --> 01:16:19.040
So they were very much concerned.

01:16:18.200 --> 01:16:26.320
Most of them were on leave or they were in the process of joining a new unit, or they had already joined.

01:16:25.480 --> 01:16:34.080
So, there were very few bodies on the ground, realistically, available to us to deal with the job.

01:16:35.120 --> 01:16:42.120
Most trains had no military escorts with inevitable consequences.

01:16:44.280 --> 01:16:55.120
John Moores was called to Ambala station to meet a train that had been attacked.

01:16:54.280 --> 01:16:57.880
This was a trainload of, I don't know, 500, 600, 700 people.

01:16:57.040 --> 01:17:00.600
Most of whom had been killed, slaughtered.

01:16:59.680 --> 01:17:07.840
Er...mainly elderly... people, young children, young women.

01:17:06.880 --> 01:17:14.000
And they had been attacked and slaughtered, shot.

01:17:13.520 --> 01:17:19.720
I can remember seeing a young woman, she was still alive, with her head open.

01:17:19.040 --> 01:17:20.760
Cut open.

01:17:20.160 --> 01:17:23.920
You could see her brains.

01:17:23.120 --> 01:17:25.800
There was nothing we could do.

01:17:24.880 --> 01:17:29.400
We hadn't got any surgical facilities or anything like that.

01:17:28.800 --> 01:17:33.960
But it was a horrendous, horrendous thing.

01:17:33.280 --> 01:17:38.880
And I still think about it very much.

01:17:38.320 --> 01:17:45.280
Art student Satish Gujral helped organise the transportation of refugees.

01:17:44.440 --> 01:17:49.480
In Amritsar, he saw the hatred Partition had brought.

01:18:14.040 --> 01:18:45.880
In the coming months, around 15 million people made the journey from one side to the other.

01:18:45.400 --> 01:18:49.560
At least one million were dead.

01:18:48.600 --> 01:19:01.240
Thousands more lay abandoned in makeshift refugee camps, stuck on the wrong side of the border.

01:19:01.920 --> 01:19:03.800
The Vicereine now lead the relief effort.

01:19:02.960 --> 01:19:10.440
My mother was for ever visiting the Punjab.

01:19:10.040 --> 01:19:17.840
She visited... in five months... she did 70 major tours of the Punjab.

01:19:17.040 --> 01:19:19.000
Into the camps.

01:19:18.320 --> 01:19:21.560
And trying to organise.

01:19:20.720 --> 01:19:22.360
Organise the camps.

01:19:21.440 --> 01:19:25.960
Organise the hospital, get guards on hospitals.

01:19:25.160 --> 01:19:28.880
Get food.

01:19:28.040 --> 01:19:40.680
And they soon learned that tell Lady Mountbatten something or answer one of her questions, and something will happen.

01:19:40.920 --> 01:19:50.040
Panditji Nehru, the new prime minister, toured some of the camps with her.

01:19:49.280 --> 01:20:01.200
Finally, he came face to face with the prices people had paid for partition.

01:20:05.280 --> 01:20:12.200
Satish Gujral was at a refugee camp in Lahore when he arrived.

01:20:11.240 --> 01:20:47.800
In Lahore, there were now few Hindus left.

01:21:01.560 --> 01:21:09.320
Som Anand and his father were among the last.

01:21:08.480 --> 01:21:18.760
In the weeks following Partition, they came under attack from Muslim gangs seeking a religiously pure Lahore.

01:21:17.960 --> 01:21:23.080
And it was free-for-all in the city.

01:21:22.160 --> 01:21:23.880
There was no one to check them.

01:21:23.200 --> 01:21:28.440
So they had come back for that purpose and somebody had given them our address and all that.

01:21:27.680 --> 01:21:39.280
And they came to kill us, but fortunately we had a very narrow escape and I was with the neighbour, with a Muslim neighbour, and they looted, ransacked the house.

01:21:38.520 --> 01:21:57.160
Took away all the clothes which they could use, all the shoes, and when our Muslim neighbour raised a hue and cry, they said, we'll shoot you if you support the kafirs.

01:21:56.360 --> 01:22:07.800
It was too much even for him and his father, who throughout the violence had held out the hope that they could stay on in the city of their birth.

01:22:07.120 --> 01:22:11.200
Then we decided to go to Delhi.

01:22:10.360 --> 01:22:11.880
Straightaway.

01:22:11.240 --> 01:22:18.800
We went to the airport and came to Delhi in our clothes and no... nothing.

01:22:17.880 --> 01:22:19.200
We left in a plane.

01:22:18.320 --> 01:22:24.080
My father bought two tickets for Delhi.

01:22:23.200 --> 01:22:28.600
I saw the other houses when the plane went up.

01:22:27.760 --> 01:22:31.240
Right over our town.

01:22:30.520 --> 01:22:34.920
I wept.

01:22:38.160 --> 01:22:51.560
In Dhillon's village, where the Muslims had been protected by his grandfather, the Indian Army arrived one day and ordered the Muslims out.

01:22:52.960 --> 01:22:54.640
It was a sort of very heavy moment.

01:22:53.800 --> 01:23:00.440
There were tearful departures and farewells.

01:22:59.840 --> 01:23:05.280
The Muslims left most of their... assets and properties with their neighbours.

01:23:04.600 --> 01:23:09.960
Thinking they would be able to come again to pick those things up.

01:23:17.360 --> 01:23:24.040
Initially, the people felt very remorse about their going.

01:23:23.200 --> 01:23:32.640
But then, in a couple of days, sort of, they tried to occupy their homes.

01:23:31.920 --> 01:23:37.160
They tried to take away whatever they had left.

01:23:43.120 --> 01:23:50.080
In only a few months, India had been divided along religious lines.

01:23:49.120 --> 01:24:02.360
The Indian part of the Punjab was cleared of nearly all its Muslims, while Pakistan was emptied of most of its Sikhs and Hindus.

01:24:01.560 --> 01:24:12.760
Lahore, once one of the most mixed cities in all of India, was now home only to Muslims.

01:24:12.760 --> 01:24:18.560
Gone was the cosmopolitan Lahore.

01:24:17.600 --> 01:24:22.680
Gone was liberal, tolerant, progressive Lahore.

01:24:21.840 --> 01:24:27.400
But I think, culturally, Lahore suffered.

01:24:26.680 --> 01:24:31.480
It was a great city in those days.

01:24:30.640 --> 01:24:35.720
It was a great city in those days.

01:24:35.080 --> 01:24:42.760
With the passage of time, we became more conservative, more backward.

01:24:42.560 --> 01:24:46.600
More intolerant.

01:24:45.640 --> 01:24:47.120
More pseudo Islamic, you know?

01:24:46.320 --> 01:24:53.000
Pseudo Islamic.

01:24:54.480 --> 01:25:04.080
I think the primary responsibility is of the Hindus who didn't treat the Muslims as their equals.

01:25:03.200 --> 01:25:04.400
That was the root cause.

01:25:03.440 --> 01:25:14.960
The Muslims and Hindus couldn't integrate despite living together for all these centuries.

01:25:14.280 --> 01:25:21.600
With the partition over, the job was done for the few British troops still left in India.

01:25:20.640 --> 01:25:27.760
John Moores returned to Britain at the end of 1947.

01:25:28.920 --> 01:25:38.040
I think the worst thing was that we couldn't stop the violence.

01:25:37.240 --> 01:25:46.600
Unnecessary cruelty is something which we didn't really know much about.

01:25:46.080 --> 01:25:54.400
Mountbatten stayed on in India until the end of 1948, as its Governor-General.

01:25:53.720 --> 01:25:59.200
He returned to his career in the Navy, becoming First Sea Lord.

01:25:58.280 --> 01:26:04.200
He was killed by an IRA bomb in 1979.

01:26:03.920 --> 01:26:12.360
The whole problem was that Mountbatten tried to do this job in too short a time.

01:26:11.480 --> 01:26:27.840
To expect a country to be partitioned and a new country to be created and within two months, move the portion of the government of India, to Pakistan, and to move the troops from Pakistan to India, was a tremendous job.

01:26:27.200 --> 01:26:29.840
It can't be done and that's exactly what happened.

01:26:28.960 --> 01:26:35.320
Everything went out of control.

01:26:37.680 --> 01:26:56.680
Satish Gujral, the art student from Lahore became one of India's greatest painters.

01:27:18.120 --> 01:27:32.840
The border created in 1947 would become the focus for three wars, and 60 years of animosity between the government of India and Pakistan.

01:27:32.120 --> 01:27:40.080
But for the people of the two countries, it was the tragedy of separation that lingered on.

01:27:43.280 --> 01:27:47.520
I missed my friends.

01:27:46.640 --> 01:27:52.360
I couldn't understand why they had to go.

01:27:51.560 --> 01:27:53.680
Why were they pushed away?

01:27:53.000 --> 01:27:56.480
The only thing was that they were being pushed away.

01:27:55.760 --> 01:27:59.680
I knew they weren't going happily.

01:27:58.960 --> 01:28:01.680
So I couldn't understand.

01:28:00.840 --> 01:28:03.160
Why?

01:28:02.360 --> 01:28:09.680
And I still think, why?

01:28:09.080 --> 01:28:12.280
What did anybody get out of it?
